A||||6||Eddie.H||VALVE COVER LEAK||||11||
Z||000000||Eddie.H||01-03-2000||09:38 PM||||HAS any replaced the valve cover gasket on the 1987 2.5 if so did try the rubber gasket or the cork gasket ?also what kind of gasket sealer did you use please help me with some info Eddie||||reg||11
Z||000001||Fieroguy1||01-04-2000||12:57 AM||||I perfer the rubber gaskets and the blue RTV <BR>gasket sealer. Just make sure all gasket surfaces are clean and have a light coating of the RTV. Let it get tacky ( approx. 15 mins.) then assemble. <BR>Good luck||||reg||14
Z||000002||Scott2M4||01-04-2000||09:16 AM||||I just replaced the gasket on my 87 2.5 last month.  I used a Felpro kit - for $8, you get a rubber gasket for both the valve cover and EGR valve.||||reg||1
Z||000003||ChadMan||01-04-2000||10:29 AM||||I used the cork gasket and orange Permatex sealer on my 88 2.5 (same engine).  35K miles and counting with no leaks.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||01-04-2000||09:00 PM||||It doesn't matter much if you are carefull, and the cover is in good shape.<P>The rubber is better for most people. It's harder to destroy durring install then the cork. It will take a little more load. If the cover has been banged up a bit the rubber tends to hold a better seal.<P>For sealer I usually use HighTack, Super300, or aviation sealer. These don't leave snot balls that can break off and plug something.<P>Most important is getting it all clean. no sealer will work on a dirty surface.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cooter||01-04-2000||09:17 PM||||One problem with either gasket is making it fit after it has been twisted up in a box for a few months. I found that spraying the gasket with a little spray adhesive (like you would glue cloth to a headliner with) will dry almost instantly and hold the gasket in place while getting the valve cover in place.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Eddie.H||01-04-2000||09:57 PM||||THANKS FOR THE INPUT GUYS I am gona go for it sunday||||reg||10
